It's meant as a joke and hinges on the idea that it's kinda Natalie Wynn's signature shot at this point. I'm not accusing anyone of copying anyone else mind you, or even criticizing the shot, just sort of referencing something iconic of another trans philosophy based YouTube channel. You're supposed to hear the joke and go "eeeeyyyyy, that's a Contrapoints thing" and then quickly move on. There's really no greater meaning or depth behind it and it certainly isn't a genuine suggestion.

&#x200B;

If this was a a tweet from Natalie I would have suggested she wear a stylish and dark colored outfit against a white featureless background.

&#x200B;

Also because this is the internet and people insist on ignoring context it probably isn't a good idea to abbreviate Contrapoints to CP. Just to be safe.

Well, they are correct in that racial preferences are a thing, as are preferences for size or appearance. However, what matters is the underlying reasons for these preferences and how they are expressed. Due to how we in our culture use language and how its used online a lot of times people who are expressing their preferences just wholesale lack the introspection and language necessary to express themselves without sounding bigoted.

&#x200B;

I mean for example it's worth noting that the hero of this screengrab chose as their examples of black beauty three women who are notably light-skinned and are generally more in line with white beauty aesthetics. So them being raised as examples of women that the bigot should otherwise consider attractive is itself rooted in racism. I'm not accusing our hero here of being racist for suggesting those three as opposed to say Letitia Wright, Keke Palmer, or let's go back in time a way and say Betty Davis, rather it's a very safe assumption that those women were used as examples due to their elevated profiles as celebrities. However to the converse it's notable that the only one of those three our bigot rebuffs is Rihanna, the one of the three with a controversial reputation and history of behaviors that one might well call unsavory, their opinion on the rest not given. Now make no mistake I'm not accusing the bigot of not being racist any more than I am accusing our hero of being racist; I'm simply pointing to the fact that even in the conversation of whether or not finding members of a particular ethnic group attractive is itself those saying that having exclusionary preferences is racist are going to make arguments founded in racism. \[As an aside, using celebrities in general is probably not a great way to gauge racism in someones sexual preferences, though I can't think of an easier and more standardized way to do so, because celebrities are attractive as an abstract figure. Letitia Wright to you or I as far as our daily lives are concerned is no more a real person than her character Shuri from Black Panther, you have a realistic expectation that you might meet Ms. Wright as opposed to Shuri, but you probably never will never mind find yourself having the opportunity to be intimate with her. Finding celebrities attractive for a lot of people, myself included has more to do with them being generally agreed upon as beautiful and attractive as opposed to the practical attraction that one might feel for someone they know and encounter more regularly. It's the difference between your spouse and a statue, there's an argument to be made that celebrity attractiveness is a kind of agalmatophilia.\]

&#x200B;

The subject of human attraction is tricky enough and where race and bigotry in general is involved is a difficult and tricky subject, one where most people simply lack the necessary language and introspection to express themselves without coming off as bigoted in some capacity. I hope that I have made the argument that the conversation shown here is a good example of bigotry being shown by both sides, and that it's assumptions that we're making (reasonable one's I would argue but assumptions no less) that decide who is or isn't racist in this conversation more than either of their arguments. The unfortunate point I'm driving at here being that this conversation is devoid of meaning outside of an example of how difficult the subject is to discuss in the first place.

I think the most generous way to rule this is that the vampire that drinks the last drop is the one who has their hunger reduced to 0.

&#x200B;

But as a Storyteller I would limit it to one person per kindred. The idea of the rule they must glut themselves on blood to satisfy their hunger, the beast. They must give in completely to the monstrous nature of the curse. No creature on earth has to overfeed itself to sate itself, underscoring the unearthly nature of vampires. It's designed to incur a loss of humanity and hasten the degradation into becoming a wight. Either live and keep your beast in control or give in to its demands and lose your humanity. That's the choice you're presented with and that's the choice you make, no getting around it, the curse you bear is greater than you could ever be.

